{"pages":["AI Coach John Follow  Repost  VOCABULARY GUIDE LOOKS SIMILAR. MEANS DIFFERENT. AI Terms That Confuse Everyone 25 AI terms you've been using interchangeably (but shouldn't)  Swipe to fix your vocabulary","AI Coach John Follow  Repost  AI VOCABULARY Latency VS Response Time  WHAT Latency: time until the first token/byte arrives. Response Time: total time until the entire response is complete.  WHY THE CONFUSION Both feel like \"how fast is it\" but one measures a starting point, the other measures a full journey.  WHEN IT MATTERS Chat UIs care about latency (feels instant). Batch jobs care about response time (total throughput).  EXAMPLE A model with 200ms latency but a 10-second response time feels fast (streaming starts quick) even though the full answer takes long.  COMMON MISTAKE Teams optimize response time and wonder why users still say the app \"feels slow\" they ignored latency.","AI Coach John Follow  Repost  AI VOCABULARY Context Window VS Memory  WHAT Context Window: the token limit the model can \"see\" in one session. Memory: information that persists across sessions.  WHY THE CONFUSION Both relate to \"what the model remembers\" but context window resets, memory doesn't.  WHEN IT MATTERS Building a chatbot? Context window decides conversation length. Memory decides if it remembers you tomorrow.  EXAMPLE ChatGPT's memory feature isn't expanding the context window it's injecting saved facts back into a fresh context window each time.  COMMON MISTAKE Assuming a bigger context window = the model \"remembers\" you. It doesn't, once the session ends.","AI Coach John Follow  Repost  AI VOCABULARY RAG VS Fine-Tuning  WHAT RAG: retrieves external knowledge at query time, no weight changes. Fine-tuning: retrains the model's weights on new data.  WHY THE CONFUSION Both are \"ways to make the model know more.\"  WHEN IT MATTERS RAG  fast-changing, factual, document-heavy knowledge. Fine-tuning  changing tone, behavior, format, or domain reasoning style.  EXAMPLE A legal chatbot needing today's case law  RAG. A chatbot needing to \"sound like a senior partner\"  fine-tuning.  COMMON MISTAKE Fine-tuning a model to \"add facts\" facts go stale; RAG handles that far better.","AI Coach John Follow  Repost  AI VOCABULARY Agent VS Workflow  WHAT Agent: decides its own next steps using reasoning. Workflow: follows a fixed, predefined sequence.  WHY THE CONFUSION Both \"automate a process.\"  WHEN IT MATTERS Predictable, repeatable task  workflow (cheaper, reliable). Open- ended, judgment-based task  agent.  EXAMPLE \"Send invoice every Friday\" = workflow. \"Investigate why revenue dropped and decide what to check next\" = agent.  COMMON MISTAKE Calling every LLM-powered automation an \"agent.\" Most are just workflows with an LLM step.","AI Coach John Follow  Repost  AI VOCABULARY Embedding VS Vector  WHAT Embedding: the process/technique of converting data into numeric representation. Vector: the output the actual array of numbers.  WHY THE CONFUSION People use them as synonyms in casual speech.  WHEN IT MATTERS Talking architecture: \"we embed the text\" (verb/process) vs \"we store the vector\" (noun/result).  EXAMPLE \"The embedding model converts text into a 1536-dimension vector\" one sentence, both terms, both correct.  COMMON MISTAKE Saying \"vector model\" instead of \"embedding model\" the model embeds; the vector is what it produces.","AI Coach John Follow  Repost  AI VOCABULARY MCP VS API  WHAT API: a general interface contract for two systems to talk. MCP (Model Context Protocol): a standardized protocol specifically for exposing tools/context to LLMs.  WHY THE CONFUSION MCP is technically built on API- like communication, so people think it's \"just another API.\"  WHEN IT MATTERS Building one-off integration  API. Building something reusable across multiple AI agents/clients  MCP.  EXAMPLE A weather API returns JSON for any app. An MCP server exposes that same data in a way any LLM client (Claude, etc.) can discover and use automatically.  COMMON MISTAKE Thinking MCP replaces APIs. It doesn't MCP often wraps APIs to make them LLM-consumable.","AI Coach John Follow  Repost  AI VOCABULARY Precision VS Recall  WHAT Precision: of what you retrieved, how much was relevant. Recall: of all relevant items that exist, how much did you retrieve.  WHY THE CONFUSION Both sound like \"accuracy\" of a retrieval system.  WHEN IT MATTERS Search engines often favor precision (top results matter). Legal/medical search favors recall (can't miss anything).  EXAMPLE Retrieving 3 docs, all relevant = high precision. But if 10 relevant docs existed, recall is low.  COMMON MISTAKE Optimizing only for precision in RAG systems leads to confident answers built on incomplete context.","AI Coach John Follow  Repost  AI VOCABULARY Tool Calling VS Function Calling  WHAT Function Calling: OpenAI's specific term/implementation for invoking a single defined function. Tool Calling: the broader concept functions, APIs, plugins, retrieval, code execution, etc.  WHY THE CONFUSION Function calling was the first popularized version, so it became the generic term.  WHEN IT MATTERS Cross-provider work Claude, Gemini, and others use \"tool calling\" as the umbrella term; \"function calling\" is narrower and OpenAI-specific.  EXAMPLE A \"tool\" can be a calculator function, a web search, or a database query function calling usually refers to just the function-style invocation.  COMMON MISTAKE Using the terms interchangeably in technical docs confuses readers across different model providers.","AI Coach John Follow  Repost  AI VOCABULARY GPT VS ChatGPT  WHAT GPT: the underlying model/architecture (e.g., GPT- 4). ChatGPT: the product the chat interface, memory, plugins, and UX wrapped around the model.  WHY THE CONFUSION Most people only ever interact with ChatGPT, so they think \"GPT\" and \"ChatGPT\" are the same thing.  WHEN IT MATTERS Developers use GPT via API (no chat UI, no memory by default). End users use ChatGPT (full product experience).  EXAMPLE You can use GPT-4 through the API with zero chat history that's not \"using ChatGPT.\"  COMMON MISTAKE Saying \"ChatGPT's API\" there is no such thing; it's the GPT model's API.","AI Coach John Follow  Repost  AI VOCABULARY Vector Database VS Knowledge Graph  WHAT Vector Database: stores embeddings, retrieves by similarity. Knowledge Graph: stores entities and explicit relationships between them.  WHY THE CONFUSION Both are \"ways to store knowledge for AI to query.\"  WHEN IT MATTERS Fuzzy, semantic similarity search  vector DB. Precise relationship reasoning (\"who reports to whom\")  knowledge graph.  EXAMPLE \"Find documents similar to this one\"  vector DB. \"Find all employees who report to someone in Finance\"  knowledge graph.  COMMON MISTAKE Using a vector DB for relationship-heavy queries it can't reliably answer multi-hop relational questions.","AI Coach John Follow  Repost  AI VOCABULARY Token VS Word  WHAT Token: the model's basic unit of text (subword chunks). Word: a human linguistic unit.  WHY THE CONFUSION Often roughly 1:1 for simple English words, so people assume they're equivalent.  WHEN IT MATTERS Pricing, context limits, and rate limits are all token-based, not word- based.  EXAMPLE \"Tokenization\" might split into \"Token\" + \"ization\" one word, two tokens.  COMMON MISTAKE Estimating costs by word count token count (often ~1.3x word count) is what actually bills you.","AI Coach John Follow  Repost  AI VOCABULARY Training VS Inference  WHAT Training: the process of teaching the model by adjusting weights. Inference: the process of using the trained model to generate output.  WHY THE CONFUSION Both involve \"the model doing something with data.\"  WHEN IT MATTERS Training happens once (or periodically); inference happens every single time you send a prompt.  EXAMPLE GPT-4 was trained over months on massive datasets. Every ChatGPT response you get is inference no learning happens.  COMMON MISTAKE Thinking the model \"learns\" from your conversation. It doesn't that's inference, not training.","AI Coach John Follow  Repost  AI VOCABULARY Fine-tuning VS Prompt Engineering  WHAT Fine-tuning: updates the model's internal weights with new examples. Prompt Engineering: crafts better inputs without touching the model at all.  WHY THE CONFUSION Both aim to \"get better outputs.\"  WHEN IT MATTERS Need quick iteration, low cost  prompt engineering. Need consistent behavior baked in at scale  fine-tuning.  EXAMPLE Adding \"respond only in bullet points\" to your prompt = prompt engineering. Training the model on 10,000 bullet-formatted examples = fine-tuning.  COMMON MISTAKE Jumping to fine-tuning before exhausting prompt engineering most problems are solvable with better prompts, cheaper and faster.","AI Coach John Follow  Repost  AI VOCABULARY Similarity Search VS Semantic Search  WHAT Similarity Search: pure vector- distance matching (cosine similarity, etc.). Semantic Search: broader system that incorporates meaning/intent, often combining similarity search with ranking, filters, or hybrid methods.  WHY THE CONFUSION Semantic search is often implemented using similarity search, so the terms blur.  WHEN IT MATTERS Designing a search system similarity search is one component; semantic search is the user-facing capability.  EXAMPLE Pure similarity search might return \"Apple the fruit\" for a query about \"Apple the company\" if vectors are close. Semantic search systems add context/ranking to avoid that.  COMMON MISTAKE Calling raw cosine-similarity retrieval \"semantic search\" it's a building block, not the whole system.","AI Coach John Follow  Repost  AI VOCABULARY Dense Retrieval VS Sparse Retrieval  WHAT Dense Retrieval: uses embeddings to find conceptually similar text. Sparse Retrieval: uses keyword/term matching (e.g., BM25, TF-IDF).  WHY THE CONFUSION Both are \"retrieval methods\" used in RAG pipelines.  WHEN IT MATTERS Conceptual/paraphrased queries  dense. Exact keyword/legal/code search  sparse. Best systems use both (hybrid retrieval).  EXAMPLE Searching \"car problems\" finding a doc about \"automobile issues\" = dense retrieval working. Searching an exact product SKU = sparse retrieval winning.  COMMON MISTAKE Going all-in on dense retrieval and losing exact-match accuracy for codes, IDs, or names.","AI Coach John Follow  Repost  AI VOCABULARY Reflection VS Self-Correction  WHAT Reflection: the model evaluates/critiques its own output. Self-Correction: the model acts on that critique to produce a better output.  WHY THE CONFUSION They're sequential steps of the same loop, so people merge them into one idea.  WHEN IT MATTERS Designing agent loops reflection without self-correction is just commentary; you need both for actual improvement.  EXAMPLE \"This answer is missing a citation\" = reflection. Regenerating the answer with the citation added = self-correction.  COMMON MISTAKE Building a \"reflection step\" that never feeds back into the generation wasted compute, no improvement.","AI Coach John Follow  Repost  AI VOCABULARY Autonomous VS Automated  WHAT Automated: follows fixed, predefined rules no decision- making. Autonomous: makes independent decisions based on context/reasoning.  WHY THE CONFUSION Both mean \"runs without a human.\"  WHEN IT MATTERS Marketing vs. reality many \"autonomous agents\" are actually just automated scripts with an LLM bolted on.  EXAMPLE A cron job emailing a report = automated. An agent that decides whether a report is even needed today = autonomous.  COMMON MISTAKE Overselling automation as autonomy sets wrong expectations for what the system can actually handle.","AI Coach John Follow  Repost  AI VOCABULARY Hallucination VS Bias  WHAT Hallucination: the model generates false/fabricated information confidently. Bias: the model's outputs are systematically skewed toward certain patterns, viewpoints, or groups.  WHY THE CONFUSION Both are \"model gets it wrong\" but one is about truth, the other about fairness/skew.  WHEN IT MATTERS Debugging output errors fact-check for hallucination, audit for bias; different fixes for each.  EXAMPLE Model citing a fake research paper = hallucination. Model consistently associating certain job titles with one gender = bias.  COMMON MISTAKE Lumping all \"wrong\" outputs under \"hallucination\" bias often needs dataset-level fixes, not fact-checking.","AI Coach John Follow  Repost  AI VOCABULARY Accuracy VS Precision  WHAT Accuracy: overall correctness across all predictions (right vs total). Precision: correctness specifically among positive predictions.  WHY THE CONFUSION Both sound like \"how often is the model right.\"  WHEN IT MATTERS Imbalanced datasets accuracy can be misleading; precision tells you a sharper story.  EXAMPLE A spam filter that marks everything \"not spam\" can have 95% accuracy (if spam is rare) but 0% precision on catching spam.  COMMON MISTAKE Reporting only accuracy on imbalanced classification tasks it hides real performance issues.","AI Coach John Follow  Repost  AI VOCABULARY Monitoring VS Observability  WHAT Monitoring: tracking known, predefined metrics (latency, error rate, token usage). Observability: the ability to debug unknown issues using logs, traces, and system internals.  WHY THE CONFUSION Both are \"watching the system.\"  WHEN IT MATTERS Monitoring tells you something broke. Observability tells you why it broke.  EXAMPLE Monitoring shows latency spiked at 2pm. Observability lets you trace it to a specific slow tool call inside an agent chain.  COMMON MISTAKE Having dashboards (monitoring) but no tracing (observability) you see the symptom, never the cause.","AI Coach John Follow  Repost  AI VOCABULARY Open Source VS Open Weight  WHAT Open Source: full transparency code, training data, methodology all available. Open Weight: only the trained model parameters are released; training data/code stay private.  WHY THE CONFUSION Both get marketed as \"open.\"  WHEN IT MATTERS Compliance, auditing, and reproducibility open weight models can't be fully audited or retrained from scratch.  EXAMPLE Llama models are open weight (you get the weights, not the full training pipeline or dataset).  COMMON MISTAKE Calling open-weight models \"open source\" a common but technically inaccurate label.","AI Coach John Follow  Repost  AI VOCABULARY Quantization VS Compression  WHAT Quantization: reduces numeric precision of weights (e.g., 32-bit  4-bit). Compression: the broader umbrella includes quantization, pruning, distillation, and more.  WHY THE CONFUSION Quantization is the most common compression technique, so people use it as a stand-in for the whole category.  WHEN IT MATTERS Choosing a model-shrinking strategy quantization is one tool, not the only one.  EXAMPLE Running a 4-bit quantized model on a laptop = quantization. Combining quantization + pruning + distillation for a mobile model = full compression pipeline.  COMMON MISTAKE Saying \"we compressed the model\" when you only quantized it leaves out other available techniques.","AI Coach John Follow  Repost  AI VOCABULARY Temperature VS Top-P  WHAT Temperature: scales the entire probability distribution to control randomness. Top-P (nucleus sampling): truncates the distribution to the smallest set of tokens whose cumulative probability hits P.  WHY THE CONFUSION Both are \"randomness knobs\" for generation.  WHEN IT MATTERS Tuning creative vs. deterministic output they interact, and tuning both without understanding each can give unpredictable results.  EXAMPLE Temperature 0.9 + Top-P 1.0 = broad, creative sampling. Temperature 0.9 + Top-P 0.3 = creative but restricted to only the most likely tokens.  COMMON MISTAKE Cranking both temperature and top-p high at once expecting more control usually just adds chaos.","AI Coach John Follow  Repost  AI VOCABULARY Structured Output VS JSON Mode  WHAT JSON Mode: guarantees the output is valid JSON but not any specific shape. Structured Output: enforces a specific schema (defined fields, types, structure).  WHY THE CONFUSION Both sound like \"the model returns clean, parseable data.\"  WHEN IT MATTERS Building production pipelines JSON mode alone can still give you wrong/missing fields; structured output enforces the exact contract your code expects.  EXAMPLE JSON mode might return {\"result\": \"yes\"} when you expected {\"status\": \"approved\", \"confidence\": 0.9}. Structured output forces that exact shape.  COMMON MISTAKE Relying on JSON mode alone for critical pipelines, then writing brittle code to handle unexpected shapes.","AI Coach John Follow  Repost  AI VOCABULARY Long-Term Memory VS Context Window  WHAT Context Window: the temporary working memory for one session. Long-Term Memory: a separate persistent store the system retrieves from and feeds back into context.  WHY THE CONFUSION Both feel like \"what the model remembers.\"  WHEN IT MATTERS Designing personalized AI products long-term memory needs its own storage + retrieval system; it doesn't just \"grow\" the context window.  EXAMPLE A coding assistant remembering your preferred code style across projects = long-term memory feeding facts back into a fresh context window each time.  COMMON MISTAKE Assuming long context window = long-term memory. Without a memory architecture, everything is forgotten once the session ends."]}